WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.260
Deaths of despair — increases in mortality
caused by suicide, addiction, and

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.660
alcoholism — is a term that describes the
crisis caused by job losses in

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.740
manufacturing and other heavy industries.
Several recent studies have suggested

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.550
that unemployment was a factor in these
deaths, especially in regions like

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:28.500
Appalachia. But a new study claims that
the real culprit may be coal mining

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.579
itself. Coal mines are associated with
injuries that often require treatment

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:37.079
with opioid painkillers. The more opioid
use, the greater the community's exposure

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:42.079
to opioids — both legal and illegal — resulting in
higher rates of addiction and death. A

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:46.200
decline in coal employment was
associated with lower rates of opioid

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:52.289
deaths. As the rate of coal employment rises,
so does opioid related mortality. Coal

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:56.520
mining is sometimes romanticized,
despite its physical difficulty and danger.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.739
Miners and industrial workers need and
deserve support after job loss, but we

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.520
shouldn't forget the risks they endure
when we hear calls to bring back coal.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.820
What do you think is the best way to
help unemployed workers? Let us know in

00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:15.850
the comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:20.740
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.939
from AEI.

